oments conjecture is due to Derksen, van den Essen and Zhao (Israel J. Math., 2017), building on their earlier moment-vanishing and integral conjectures. No prior counterexample appears in the literature; the conjecture's interest was precisely that it implied the Jacobian conjecture, which was disproved on 2026-07-19. The two-variable case GMC(2) is explicitly left open by this paper.\n\nCaveats\n\narXiv preprint, not peer-reviewed, single author. The constructions are explicit polynomials and checkable by computer algebra, which lowers the cost of verification, and vibemathed has since re-derived both counterexamples in exact arithmetic for the first ten moments, which confirms the objects but not the argument that the identity holds for every m. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nau:Wang_G in:math - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?q=au%3AWang_G+in%3Amath)\nciteturn14search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Gao and Geroldinger conjectured that every minimal zero-sum sequence of length $\\mathsf D(G)$~\\cite[Conjecture~6.1]GG99 and every zero-sumfree sequence of length $\\mathsf D(G)-1$~\\cite[Conjecture~4.1]GG06 contains an element of order $\\exp(G)$. ... @misc{2610.40032, author = {Guoqing Wang and Linlin Wang and Jun Yue}, title = {{S}ubmodular decompositions: a bounded-variation counterexample and an exact planar cut formula}, year = {2026}, eprint = {2610.40032}, note = {arXiv:2610.40032v2} }[Button: Copy Citation] ... Frank, Yuxin Ge, Guofang Wang, Wei Wei ... The existence of $C=16/5$ also brings us closer to the most challenging conjecture on the Davenport constant, namely the Emde Boas--Kruyswijk Conjecture in 1969, which predicts that $\\DD(C_n^r)=r(n-1)+1.\n\n0\n\n@misc{2611.02063, author = {Yongke Qu and Guoqing Wang}, title = {{E}xtremal zero-sum sequences without elements of maximal order}, year = {2026}, eprint = {2611.02063}, note = {arXiv:2611.02063v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nLet $\\mathsf D(G)$ denote the Davenport constant of a finite abelian group $G$, the maximal length of a minimal zero-sum sequence over $G$. Gao and Geroldinger conjectured that every minimal zero-sum sequence of length $\\mathsf D(G)$~\\cite[Conjecture~6.1]GG99 and every zero-sumfree sequence of length $\\mathsf D(G)-1$~\\cite[Conjecture~4.1]GG06 contains an element of order $\\exp(G)$. We disprove both conjectures by proving a density-one theorem: for every fixed finite abelian group $H$ of exponent $M=2^ap^b$, where $a\\ge2$, $b\\ge1$, and $p$ is an odd prime, the set of integers $r\\ge0$ for which $G_r=C_2^r\\oplus H$ admits a minimal zero-sum sequence of length $\\mathsf D(G_r)$ with no term of order $M$ has natural density one.\n\n  * Infinite Generalized Sidon Sets with a Negative Answer to a Problem of Szemerédi\nMore generally, we show that $m(k,b)=\\Theta(kb)$ for all sufficiently large odd $k$ and every integer $b$ with $k\\leq b\\leq (k-1)(k-3)/2$, where the implied constants are independent of $k$ and $b$.\n\n  * The equality $s(G)=\\eta(G)+\\exp(G)-1$ fails for $G=C_2^7\\oplus C_6$\n\nGuoqing Wang, Xiangneng Zeng\n\nNov 03 2026 math.CO arXiv:2611.01972v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2611.01972, author = {Guoqing Wang and Xiangneng Zeng}, title = {{T}he equality $s({G})=\\eta({G})+\\exp({G})-1$ fails for ${G}={C}_2^7\\oplus {C}_6$}, year = {2026}, eprint = {2611.01972}, note = {arXiv:2611.01972v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nLet $G$ be a finite abelian group. The Erdős-Ginzburg-Ziv constant $\\mathsf{s}(G)$ is the smallest positive integer $\\ell$ such that every sequence over $G$ of length at least $\\ell$ contains a zero-sum subsequence of length $\\exp(G)$. The short zero-sum constant $\\eta(G)$ is defined similarly, with the requirement that the zero-sum subsequence is nonempty and has length at most $\\exp(G)$. It is known that $\\mathsf{s}(G)\\ge \\eta(G)+\\exp(G)-1.$ A conjecture proposed by W. Gao in 2003, which has been widely studied in zero-sum theory, predicts that $$ \\mathsfs(G)=\\eta(G)+\\exp(G)-1 $$ for every nontrivial finite abelian group $G$. In this paper, we prove that this conjecture fails for the group $C_2^7\\oplus C_6$.\n\n  * Submodular decompositions: a bounded-variation counterexample and an exact planar cut formula\n\nGuoqing Wang, Linlin Wang, Jun Yue\n\nOct 30 2026 math.CO arXiv:2610.40032v2\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2610.40032, author = {Guoqing Wang and Linlin Wang and Jun Yue}, title = {{S}ubmodular decompositions: a bounded-variation counterexample and an exact planar cut formula}, year = {2026}, eprint = {2610.40032}, note = {arXiv:2610.40032v2} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nBérczi, Gehér, Imolay, Lovász, and Schwarcz (SIAM J. Discrete Math. 40(2026), 308--335) asked whether every set function of bounded variation is a difference of two bounded submodular functions, and how to determine the optimum in a monotonic sum-decomposition of a planar graph cut function. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMem. Diﬀerential Equations Math. Phys. 70 (2017), 1–6 (https://www.emis.de/journals/MDEMP/vol70/vol70-1.pdf)\nciteturn14search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; 35 (1979), 727–785. ... Postnikov). ... Morse and Maslov indices for the extremals of controllable systems (with A.\nA. Agrachev).\nMath. USSR, Sb. 35 (1979), 727–785.\n28. Chronological algebras and nonstationary vector ﬁelds (with A. A. Agrachev).\n(Russian)\nProblems in geometry, Vol. 11 (Russian), pp. 135–176, 243, Akad. Nauk SSSR, Vsesoyuz.\nInst. Nauchn. i Tekhn. Informatsii, Moscow, 1980.\n29. Diﬀerential geometric and group theoretic methods in optimal control theory (with A. A.\nAgrachev and S. A. Vakhrameev).\n(Russian) Problems in geometry, Vol.\n14, 3–56, Itogi\nNauki i Tekhniki, Akad. Nauk SSSR, Vsesoyuz. Inst. Nauchn. i Tekhn. Inform., Moscow,\n1983.\n30. The mathematical work of L. S. Pontryagin (w